    "Gunung Karangetang in North Sulawesi erupted twice on November 22, causing lava flows to extend up to 400 meters.",
    "Mount Semeru remains at Alert Level III following multiple recorded eruptions during the recent period.",
    "Authorities have maintained safety warnings for both areas and advised citizens to avoid designated hazard zones."
  ],
  "aiContextParagraph": "Recent seismic observations in Indonesia indicate concurrent volcanic activity across multiple regions. In North Sulawesi, Gunung Karangetang on Siau Island experienced two eruptions on November 22, which included the discharge of glowing lava reaching distances of approximately 400 meters. Officials at the Volcanic Observation Post have closely monitored these developments to ensure public safety in the surrounding Kabupaten Kepulauan Sitaro. Simultaneously, Mount Semeru in East Java continues to exhibit volcanic activity, with reports of several eruptions occurring over recent days. The site is currently maintained under the Level III (Siaga) status, which signifies a high level of alert for the surrounding communities. These events are part of ongoing volcanic monitoring across the archipelago, with additional activity previously reported at Mount Ibu, further reflecting the active geological conditions currently influencing these specific Indonesian regions.",
